saved accuracy-vs-layer comparison
CAPTION: Accuracy falls as noise sigma rises; the shaded region is privacy-
infeasible (leakage above budget). The optimizer picks the smallest feasible sigma.
CAPTION: Higher power shortens transmission, reducing deadline truncation and
raising accuracy until it saturates. Curves shift with the added noise.
CAPTION: As the privacy budget tightens (right to left), the optimizer moves the
split deeper AND raises noise — the two privacy levers working together.
CAPTION: With the per-element budget, the minimum feasible noise is the same at
every layer (flat line) — this is why the budget makes layers comparable.
CAPTION: Accuracy as a joint function of split layer and noise. The star marks
the PA-BSE operating point; accuracy degrades sharply at high noise.
CAPTION: How deadline-induced truncation degrades accuracy; deeper layers are
more robust because their smaller activations survive truncation better.
\nAll pairwise plots saved. 6 relationship plots + 1 heatmap.
CAPTION: Accuracy falls as noise increases; shaded region is privacy-infeasible (below the per-element budget floor). The optimizer picks the smallest feasible sigma.
CAPTION: Higher power raises the transmission rate, reducing deadline truncation and thus raising feasible accuracy, up to the energy limit.
CAPTION: Under the per-element budget, the minimum feasible noise is the SAME at every split layer (flat line) — this is exactly why the per-element formulation makes the split-point comparison fair. (With a total budget this line would slope.)
CAPTION: As the budget tightens (right to left), the optimizer moves the split DEEPER and increases noise — trading compute for privacy. The key coupling result.
CAPTION: Accuracy vs the fraction of the activation dropped when the deadline binds. Deeper layers tolerate truncation differently — this is A(l,j).
CAPTION: Joint accuracy landscape. Left of the dashed line is privacy-infeasible; the optimizer lands at the star — the highest-accuracy feasible point.

